Yes, a genetic algorithm (GA) could help, particularly if lsqnonlin is converging to a poor local minimum. But the sequence you’re seeing—

1000 \rightarrow 820 \rightarrow 815 \rightarrow 815

—doesn’t necessarily prove that the optimizer is trapped in a local minimum. It could also be stopping because of a poorly conditioned Jacobian, weak sensitivity to the drag multipliers, or excessive regularization.

Why GA might work better

Your current problem optimizes a vector of drag multipliers:

\mathbf{s}=s_1,s_2,\\ldots,s_N^T

lsqnonlin uses local derivative information to improve that vector. GA instead explores a population of candidate multiplier vectors, making it less dependent on a particular starting point.

However, GA is considerably more computationally expensive and doesn’t guarantee finding the global minimum. MATLAB +1

What I’d recommend: GA followed by lsqnonlin

This is a hybrid optimization strategy:

* GA searches broadly for a promising multiplier history.
* lsqnonlin refines the solution using local least-squares optimization.
* Your existing adaptive knot refinement can remain unchanged.

You can test this by replacing your initial lsqnonlin call with:

% Existing residual function
fun = @(x) residual(x,knots,run,dt);
% NEW: Genetic algorithm
n = numel(x0);
lb = 0.85*ones(n,1);
ub = 1.15*ones(n,1);
fitness = @(x) sum(fun(x(:)).^2);
gaOpts = optimoptions('ga', ...
    'Display','iter', ...
    'PopulationSize',40, ...
    'MaxGenerations',30);
[xGA,fGA] = ga(fitness,n,[],[],[],[], ...
               lb,ub,[],gaOpts);
% NEW: Refine the GA solution using lsqnonlin
x = lsqnonlin(fun,xGA(:),lb,ub,opts);

This requires Global Optimization Toolbox for ga and Optimization Toolbox for lsqnonlin. Both solvers are available in MATLAB R2021b.

Important: GA requires a scalar objective, so we minimize the sum of squared residuals. This is equivalent to what lsqnonlin was minimizing. MATLAB

Before doing that, I’d run one diagnostic

Inside your fitting function, after defining fun, evaluate:

for s = [0.90 0.95 1.00 1.05 1.10]
    J = sum(fun(s*ones(size(x0))).^2);
    fprintf('Scale %.2f: Cost %.4f\n',s,J);
end

This will show how the objective responds to five different constant drag scales.

* If a scale of 0.95 gives a cost of 500 while your optimizer stopped at 815, that’s strong evidence the local optimization needs investigation.
* If every scale gives approximately 815, the trajectory may have low sensitivity to these corrections, or the objective may be dominated by errors that drag scaling cannot fix.

Also inspect the lsqnonlin exit message, because stopping due to small changes is different from demonstrating a good optimum.

My recommendation for your current implementation

I’d first try GA + lsqnonlin using 10–20 initial knots, then allow your existing adaptive refinement to increase the knot count.

I would not initially run GA over 50–60 independent multipliers. The search space grows substantially, and every fitness evaluation requires a trajectory propagation.

That combination gives you a broader initial search while preserving almost all of the MATLAB code we’ve already developed.

One final point: if GA also consistently stops around 815, I would investigate whether the remaining error comes from missing lift, gravity-model differences, numerical integration, or trajectory timing rather than assuming the optimizer is failing.
